Thank you for this quick answer !

I choose a buffer width of 1900m and it seems to be enough.

> SCR.naive2 = secr.fit(capturedata, buffer = 1900, trace = FALSE)
> suggest.buffer(SCR.naive2)
[1] 1902

The esa.plot confirms it too. I chose a spacing in order to have 1124 points restricted to the forest habitat.

So my next issue is on the choice of the detection function even if I know it's not determinant. According to AICc, the exponential one is the best but the esa.plot (in attached files) with the three functions seems to indicate that density doesn't reach a plateau for a buffer width of 1900m.

> AIC(dfn.fits)
                   model                          detectfn      npar    logLik            AIC             AICc         dAICc     AICcwt
EXP     D~1 g0~1 sigma~1         exponential    3   -172.7673    351.535     356.335     0.000      0.8481
HAZ    D~1 g0~1 sigma~1 z~1 hazard rate    4    -171.4862    350.972     360.972     4.637      0.0835
HN      D~1 g0~1 sigma~1          halfnormal    3    -175.2857    356.571     361.371     5.036      0.0684
> suggest.buffer(SCRhalf)
[1] 1902
> suggest.buffer(SCRhaz)
[1] 3187
> suggest.buffer(SCRexp)
[1] 2907

Should I keep the half normal or increase the buffer width to use the exponential ? The rank of the exponential seems consistent with mountainous and forest terrain for me

